MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Some further detail. I have now estimated four models separately and extracted the scores. Here is the correlation matrix for these scores using EAP:

## 4 separate 1-dimensional models
> cor(data.frame(Conserve=scores.F1$F1, Openness=scores.F2$F1,
+                Transcend=scores.F3$F1, Enhance=scores.F4$F1))
          Conserve Openness Transcend  Enhance
Conserve   1.00000  -0.1442   0.60796 -0.05301
Openness  -0.14424   1.0000   0.12303  0.63539
Transcend  0.60796   0.1230   1.00000  0.06148
Enhance   -0.05301   0.6354   0.06148  1.00000

Now here is the correlations from EAP and MAP as calculated from the confirmatory model specified above:

## Single, 4-dimensional model
> cor(scoresEAP)
          Conserve Openness Transcend  Enhance
Conserve   1.00000 -0.14201   0.51987 -0.06961
Openness  -0.14201  1.00000   0.09235  0.53992
Transcend  0.51987  0.09235   1.00000  0.03350
Enhance   -0.06961  0.53992   0.03350  1.00000

> cor(scoresMAP)
          Conserve Openness Transcend  Enhance
Conserve    1.0000  -0.1451   0.60654 -0.05390
Openness   -0.1451   1.0000   0.12147  0.63362
Transcend   0.6065   0.1215   1.00000  0.06092
Enhance    -0.0539   0.6336   0.06092  1.00000

Notice that the correlations between the scores for the 4 separate models (estimated with EAP) are almost identical to MAP scores estimated from the combined model. The EAP for the combined model are a bit different, and when graphed they still have that same box-like pattern as shown before. That is not the case with EAP scores estimated from the 4 separate models.

Thanks,
~Jason

